lasched1# Names that m4 -D cannot reach, so they are renamed on m4's output instead.
#
# The siever kernels build their own names by concatenation: lasched1.asm
# writes `lasched`'ot` and m4 splices in the variant number, so the literal
# text handed to m4 is `lasched`, not `lasched1`.  Renaming `lasched` on the
# m4 command line renames the fragment and yields laschedI111; renaming
# `lasched1` there does nothing at all, because that token only exists after
# expansion.  So the first two lines of the rule below are wrong either way
# and the real work happens in the sed that follows the pipe.
#
# sed anchors both ends with \b, so a name m4 -D already suffixed (no longer a
# whole word) is left alone and the two mechanisms cannot collide.

